No. For what I am actually deciding to wear or for what I am deciding
to carry in the open inventory there's the small(?) possibility that
it may be subject to a possibly effective shock attack. You're writing
"*the* wand of death"; if your character has only one of those and
needs to _rely_ on it - i.e. if your character cannot survive without
one - you have to take precautions. Reflection is the powerful option
to reduce efforts against shock (and other ray) attacks to a minimum.
(Side note: even with reflection carried wands and rings are subject
to shock traps on boxes, AFAIK.)

For the early game there's the typical problem that you don't know what
those rings are; having found interesting rings and having identified
them and having found a source of reflection is not something I am
guaranteed until the Castle. Usefulness decreases after the early game.

Since there's no doubt about the "usefulness" (to say the least) of
reflection the question is more; what to do if you lack reflection?
Is it worth a wish? It depends, I think. Though (just from personal
experience, YMMV) I have doubts about the "necessity" of reflection.
In most cases I have a couple backups of rings, in my stashes and bag.
There are effectively no rings I can think of that I would *have* to
wear *continuously* to _survive_ in the mid/late game.
 From the rings I think the most interesting for an advanced game are,
maybe, stealth, warning, increase damage, free action, levitation,
regeneration, searching, slow digestion, conflict, polymorph control,
teleport control. For some (as with the rings of foo-resistance) there
are replacements existing. The rest of the group is small; conflict
may be considered crucial, if your character isn't proficient enough
and has no powerful pets or the possibility to charm monsters, that is.
And lacking free action might bring you in a critical situation[*].

Janis

[*] There once had been mentioned here that also free action is of
